WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.414
Digital signatures are part of the quantum threat discussion because they protect identity, integrity, authenticity, and trust.

2
00:00:08.414 --> 00:00:21.756
A signature can prove that a software update came from the expected publisher, that firmware was approved, that a certificate was issued by a trusted authority, or that a document was not changed after signing.

3
00:00:21.756 --> 00:00:25.319
These uses are deeply embedded in security operations.

4
00:00:25.319 --> 00:00:28.351
Certificate chains are especially important.

5
00:00:28.351 --> 00:00:33.354
A certificate chain links identities to public keys through trusted issuers.

6
00:00:33.354 --> 00:00:46.696
If the signature algorithms used in those chains become unsuitable, organizations need a migration path for certificate authorities, clients, servers, devices, verification libraries, and policy rules.

7
00:00:46.696 --> 00:00:49.122
That migration is not only technical.

8
00:00:49.122 --> 00:00:55.186
It may involve contracts, audits, governance, procurement, and operational change windows.

9
00:00:55.186 --> 00:01:00.644
Code signing and firmware signing can be harder than changing an encryption setting.

10
00:01:00.644 --> 00:01:03.373
Devices may validate signatures offline.

11
00:01:03.373 --> 00:01:07.921
Boot chains may depend on algorithms burned into firmware or hardware.

12
00:01:07.921 --> 00:01:13.152
Update systems may require compatibility across multiple product generations.

13
00:01:13.152 --> 00:01:24.068
Some devices stay deployed for years in medical, industrial, automotive, defense, or infrastructure environments where patching is slow and replacement is expensive.

14
00:01:24.068 --> 00:01:28.768
The practical question is where trust depends on long-lived signatures.

15
00:01:28.768 --> 00:01:39.759
Signed logs, software packages, firmware images, documents, certificates, and device identities may all have different validity periods and operational constraints.

16
00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:46.051
Signature migration requires understanding verification behavior, not just signing behavior.

17
00:01:46.051 --> 00:01:51.357
A new signing algorithm helps only if every relying system can verify it correctly.
